.TH GPU 3
.SH NAME
gpu \- VirtIO GPU VirGL command transport
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind '#∇' /mnt/gpu

.BI /mnt/gpu/ clone
.BI /mnt/gpu/ caps
.BI /mnt/gpu/ n /ctl
.BI /mnt/gpu/ n /cmd
.BI /mnt/gpu/ n /resource
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The experimental
.I gpu
device provides off-screen VirGL rendering on the first VirtIO GPU PCI
device. It requires the modern VirtIO interface and VirGL feature.
It is included in the amd64 pc64 kernel.
It does not change the display controller or the draw protocol.
.PP
Opening
.B clone
for reading and writing creates a context and returns its
.B ctl
file.
Only the host owner may open context files.
Reading
.B ctl
returns three decimal numbers: directory number, host context identifier,
and last allocated resource identifier (initially zero).
Context and resource identifiers are allocated by the kernel and are not reused.
Closing the last open file in a context releases its resources and the context.
.PP
.B caps
contains the binary little-endian VirGL capability set 1, version 1.
Applications must consult its format and feature masks before using
optional rendering features.
.PP
Each write to
.B cmd
submits one complete VirGL command stream of little-endian 32-bit words.
It must be nonempty, a multiple of four bytes, and no larger than 256 KiB.
The kernel supplies the context identifier and waits for a GPU fence
before returning.
Shader translation and VirGL command validation are performed by the host.
.PP
The following text commands are accepted by
.BR ctl .
Numbers may be decimal, octal, or hexadecimal.
.TP
.BI resource " target format bind width height depth array levels samples flags bytes"
Create a VirGL resource, allocate zeroed guest backing memory, and attach
it to the context.
The fields correspond to VirtIO RESOURCE_CREATE_3D;
.I levels
is the highest mip level, so zero means one level.
.I bytes
is the size of its guest backing store.
The new resource identifier appears in
.BR ctl .
A binary file with that decimal identifier appears in the context directory.
Reads and writes of this file access guest backing memory only;
they do not implicitly transfer data to or from the GPU.
.TP
.BI upload " id level x y z width height depth offset stride layerstride"
Transfer a box from guest backing to the GPU resource.
.I offset
is a byte offset in the backing store.
Zero strides request the host's tightly packed layout.
.TP
.BI download " id level x y z width height depth offset stride layerstride"
Transfer a box from the GPU resource to guest backing.
The application can then read the resource file.
.TP
.BI unref " id"
Detach and release a resource.
Existing descriptors for that resource become invalid, even if its table
slot is later reused.
.PP
Control operations, command submission and backing access are serialized.
All device requests are fenced.
A command timeout resets and disables the device before releasing DMA memory;
subsequent rendering requires rebooting the guest.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/9/pc/devgpu.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw (3)
.SH BUGS
This is an experimental privileged interface, not an OpenGL implementation.
It supports one GPU, 32 contexts, 64 resources and 64 MiB of backing per context.
There is one synchronous control queue; asynchronous submission, scanout,
cursor queues, buffer sharing and device recovery are not implemented.
.PP
Resource targets are 0 through 8. Buffers are limited to 64 MiB;
texture dimensions to 16384, array size to 2048, highest mip level to 14,
and sample count to 64. The host may impose lower limits.
Backing size and transfer layout must match the resource format.
Guest backing limits do not bound the host GPU memory consumed by a resource.
Only buffers and single-level BGRA images have been exercised so far.
.PP
QEMU does not propagate all VirGL renderer errors to the guest.
A successful write confirms transport completion, not a valid rendering result.
